  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Minnesota has recorded $1.4B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state saw 24,221 awards, with an average award size of $59.8K, and obligations fell 41.8% year over year. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $460M, followed by the Department of Veterans Affairs with $246M and the Department of Agriculture with $244M. NORTHROP GRUMMAN SYSTEMS CORPORATION leads vendors with $185M, and Ammunition (except Small Arms) Manufacturing is the largest industry sector at $185M. Construction and food manufacturing also show up strongly: Commercial and Institutional Building Construction received $126M, and Cheese Manufacturing received $96.5M. The General Services Administration, Department of Health and Human Services, and other agencies account for a combined presence across procurement categories, while local contractors such as MCGOUGH CONSTRUCTION CO., LLC and AGILITI HEALTH INC appear among top performers. These figures are partial, reflecting fiscal year to date activity rather than a full fiscal year total.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What changed in federal obligations to Minnesota in FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 41.8% year over year. Total obligations in FY2026 to date are $1.4B, down from prior full fiscal year totals like $2.5B in FY25 and $3B in FY24. The drop shows up across award volume and value, with 24,221 awards and an average award size of $59.8K in the current fiscal year to date."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and industries are driving federal spending in Minnesota?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $460M, making it the largest buying agency, followed by the Department of Veterans Affairs at $246M and the Department of Agriculture at $244M. Ammunition (except Small Arms) Manufacturing leads industries at $185M, with Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $126M and Cheese Manufacturing at $96.5M rounding out the top sectors. Other agencies such as the General Services Administration ($141M) and the Department of Health and Human Services ($125M) also contributed significant obligations."
      }
    ]
  },
